%I A398935 #12 Sep 21 2026 18:54:14
%S A398935 0,1,2,3,4,5,6,8,9,10,12,13,15
%N A398935 Least number of reversals of increasing or decreasing runs sufficient to sort any permutation of [n].
%C A398935 Equivalently, the least number of reversals of contiguous increasing or decreasing runs needed to reach every permutation of [n] from the identity permutation.
%C A398935 a(n-1) <= a(n) <= a(n-1)+2.
%C A398935 Proof: If n is inserted in a permutation of [n-1] which requires a(n-1) reversals to sort, the numbers 1..n-1 still require at least a(n-1) reversals, thus a(n) >= a(n-1). If a permutation of [n] begins with x, one could sort the other n-1 entries with at most a(n-1) reversals giving (x, 1, ..., x-1, x+1, ..., n). This can be sorted in at most two more reversals: (1, ..., x-1) then (x, ..., 1), thus a(n) <= a(n-1)+2.
%C A398935 The number of permutations of [n] actually requiring a(n) reversals is A399896(n).
%C A398935 Similar to A058986 (the pancake numbers).
%e A398935 For n=4, the permutation 2413 requires 3 reversals; for example, 24(13) -> 2(431) -> (21)34 -> 1234. Every permutation of [4] can be sorted in 3 such reversals or fewer, so a(4)=3.
%e A398935 For n=5, the permutation 24153 requires 4 reversals; for example, 241(53) -> 24(13)5 -> 2(431)5 -> (21)345 -> 12345. Every permutation of [5] can be sorted in 4 such reversals or fewer, so a(5)=4.
%Y A398935 Cf. A399896, A058986.
%K A398935 nonn,hard,more,new
%O A398935 1,3
%A A398935 _Eric Fox_, Sep 15 2026
